# 9 Core Principles

## 1. Observe Before Explaining
Record reality before forming conclusions. Good understanding begins with careful observation.

## 2. Reveal the Infrastructure
Every visible tool depends on invisible systems. Always seek to understand the underlying mechanisms rather than only the interface.

## 3. Follow the Patterns
Individual events are interesting. Recurring patterns become knowledge.

## 4. Build for Longevity
Write about mechanisms, incentives, and principles rather than temporary products or interfaces. The work should remain useful beyond today's technologies.

## 5. Separate Observation from Interpretation
Facts, inferences, hypotheses, and opinions should remain clearly distinguishable. Credibility depends on disciplined thinking.

## 6. Prefer Stewardship over Consumption
Knowledge should increase independence, resilience, and responsible participation in the systems we rely upon. Understanding carries responsibility.

## 7. Earn Every Principle
No idea becomes part of the Handbook simply because it sounds compelling. Principles emerge from repeated observation, research, and refinement.

## 8. Share to Learn
Publishing is part of the learning process. Ideas improve through explanation, discussion, and constructive challenge.

## 9. Preserve What Endures
The Handbook is not an archive of events. It is a curated collection of principles that continue to explain the world as it changes.
